# Emery Plugin

## Overview

This plugin provides the three Emery skills: generate a specification, seal it in the target's repository, and build its plan. Each skill is an ultrathin wrapper that invokes the matching `emery` CLI verb and relays its output.

The v1 delivery workflow (plan / refine / execute / status / finalize and the `system-*` definition loop) is archived at git tag `v1`. Its verbs are deleted from the CLI grammar and its skills are deleted from this plugin — do not attempt to run them or reconstruct their behavior by hand. `emery specify` generates `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` from the sources named on the invocation and stages them as one candidate; `emery apply` seals the candidate as the current revision in the target's repository, under `spec/` at one commit labelled `emery/<revision>`; `emery show spec`, `emery show design`, and `emery show plan` print the reviewable documents of the current revision, and with `--staged` the candidate's; `emery build` builds the sealed plan in waves through one target adapter, and lands the result as one merge commit per slice under the same label, verified wave by wave.

## Workflow

```text
/emery:specify <adapter>           (every revision: stage the candidate)
/emery:apply                       (once the candidate is reviewed: seal it)
/emery:build [<adapter>]           (once the revision is sealed)
```

That is the whole shipped surface. An `<adapter>` is an exact package reference with a version, `namespace:name@version` (`emery:typescript@0.13.0`); the binary reads it from its store, `~/.emery/adapters`, and fetches a release the store lacks on the first run that names it. The source list never persists — repeat the adapters on every run, or keep them in an operator-owned `emery.toml` selected with `--config [<path>]` (`--config` alone means the project-relative `emery.toml`; a run naming no sources at all discovers the project-root file as a fallback; adapters named beside `--config` layer over the file's entries, one of the same name taking that entry's place and any other appended). Every run starts from its sources alone — nothing of the sealed revision reaches the synthesis, requirements number from `REQ-001` in source order, and a second run replaces the candidate. After each run the skill writes `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` (the Markdown projections of the candidate, `emery show <artifact> --staged`) beside the code for review. Never hand-edit `.emery/storage` state (the staged candidate); route through the CLI.

`/emery:apply` seals the candidate where the `[target]` table of the same `emery.toml` says the specification lives — its `repository`, or `--repository <url|.>` in its place: a clone of a URL kept at `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>`, the project's own repository when it is `"."`, or, with none named, a repository the engine creates at that path on the first seal — as three documents under `spec/` in one commit `Revision <id>` over the repository's head, labelled `emery/<revision>`; the checkout is never written, nothing is pushed, and the candidate is cleared. `emery specify --config --apply` does both in one run when the operator asks to seal unreviewed. `spec/` in that repository is the engine's: a document edited there, or an operator's own `spec/`, fails every later verb closed (`spec-mismatch`), and `emery show` prints the specification instead.

`/emery:build` names one target adapter the same way, or takes it from the `[target]` table, an adapter named beside `--config` taking the place of the table's. It reads the sealed revision from the `[target]` repository — never the candidate: a build with only a candidate staged is refused (`spec-not-generated`, naming it) until `/emery:apply` seals it — and builds the plan in waves — every slice whose dependencies are merged at once, each in its own working copy, up to `--jobs` at a time — in that repository: a clone of a URL built from its default branch; the project's own, from its sealed head, when it is `"."`; or, with none named, the repository `apply` created. The engine has the adapter scaffold its layout once over the `Revision` commit, sealed as `Initialise`, seals each slice's changes as one commit, merges them in id order into an integration working copy under the adapter's merge rules — a path the rules leave conflicted is resolved with the model from both sides, the resolution sealed in the slice's working copy over its commit and the slice merged again with it taken whole — has the adapter verify the integrated tree, and labels the verified head `emery/<revision>` there; nothing is pushed. A conflict the model cannot resolve ends the run (`merge-unresolved`), as a wave the adapter does not verify does (`verify-failed`), the label standing at the last verified wave, and a re-run resumes from that label and from the slices the integration working copy holds merged over it rather than starting over, verifying them in their wave with no build turn spent; a turn the model backend's time budget ends is put once more before its failure is the run's. The checkout is never written and the skill commits nothing: it relays the base, the repository, the waves, the merges, and the label, and offers `git log --first-parent`, `git merge`, or a push of the label as the way to take them. A checkout with an uncommitted change outside `.emery/` is refused before any slice is built into it (`base-not-sealed`), so `.emery/` belongs in `.gitignore`. The projections stay `/emery:specify`'s to write, `spec/` stays `/emery:apply`'s (a build that rewrites it through the model's shell is refused, `spec-rewritten`), and the build never commits either.

## Tracing and output

One tracing level governs a run — the runtime's host console, the engine, and every adapter alike. A bare run is `info`: the engine's progress at its slow steps and each adapter's seam turn as it opens reach stderr, retries at WARN. Each `-v` raises the level one step and each `-q` lowers it — `-q` warn (progress gone, warnings kept), `-qq` error, `-qqq` off; `-v` debug, `-vv` trace — before or after the verb, and `-v` beside `-q` is a usage error. The flags override a process `RUST_LOG`; a bare run keeps one that is set, so `RUST_LOG=emery_sdk=debug emery specify …` refines a bare run and `RUST_LOG=off` silences it.

**Debug mode**: when the operator explicitly asks for debug (says "debug", or passes `debug` on the slash command), pass `-v` to **every** `emery` subprocess in that skill invocation. A subprocess that must be fully silent passes `-qqq` (or keeps `RUST_LOG=off`) so it does not inherit a raised filter.

Stream roles: the semantic CLI result (text or JSON envelope) is stdout; command failures and live tracing are stderr. Relay the semantic result or structured error once, verbatim; do not repeat or reformat tracing lines in the closing response.

## Troubleshooting

### Common error codes

Every CLI failure prints a kebab-case error code (and usually a `hint:` line — follow it). The codes met most often:

| Error code | What it means | Next action |
|------------|---------------|-------------|
| `specify-source-required` | `emery specify` was run without naming a source, and no project-root `emery.toml` was found | `emery specify <adapter>` (and/or `--description <adapter>=<text>`, or `--config [<path>]`) |
| `build-target-required` | `emery build` was run without naming a target adapter, or `emery apply`, `emery specify --apply`, or a bare `emery show` without a `[target]` table in `--config` or the project-root `emery.toml` to say where the specification lives | `emery build <adapter>`, or add a `[target]` table to `emery.toml` — its `repository` a URL, `"."`, or omitted — and pass `--config` |
| `nothing-staged` | `emery apply` or `emery show --staged` was run with no candidate in the revision store | `/emery:specify <adapter>` first |
| `adapter-reference` | An adapter was named without a version or a namespace, or as a path or bare name | Name the exact package reference, `namespace:name@version` |
| `unsupported-version` | The installed binary is older than an adapter's declared minimum `emery-version` | Reinstall through the binary's install channel (the hint prints the literal command), then rerun |
| `spec-not-generated` | `emery show` or `emery build` found no sealed revision in the `[target]` repository: none yet, a candidate staged and not sealed (the message names it), a repository with no commit, or a label `emery/<revision>` the head moved on without | `/emery:apply` for a staged candidate; `/emery:specify <adapter>` then `/emery:apply` otherwise; for a label behind the head, merge or rebase it onto the head, or `/emery:specify --apply` to seal a fresh revision over it |
| `spec-mismatch` | `spec/` in the `[target]` repository is not the revision its label names: a document edited there, or the operator's own `spec/` where the first seal would land | Restore the labelled documents (`git checkout emery/<revision> -- spec/`) or move the directory aside, then rerun; never edit the files, `emery show` prints the specification |
| `spec-rewritten` | A slice's build, a merge's resolution, or a wave's repairs changed a path under `spec/` — the sealed specification, which the model's shell alone can reach — so nothing of that change set was sealed | Inspect the named paths in `.emery/vcs/worktrees/<slice>` or `.emery/vcs/integration`, stop what wrote them, then rerun; the label stands at the last verified wave |
| `repository-required` | `emery apply`, `emery build`, or a run that reads the `[target]` was run from a directory that is no git repository, with `[target] repository = "."` naming the project checkout as where the specification lives | `git init` and seal a first commit, name a repository URL as `repository` instead, or drop the key so `apply` creates a repository of its own under `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>` |
| `base-not-sealed` | The project checkout holds an uncommitted change outside `.emery/` and `.git/`, or has no commit, so there is no sealed base to build from | Commit or stash the paths the message lists (add `.emery/` to `.gitignore`), then rerun |
| `revision-not-found` | A `[[source]] revision` names nothing its repository holds, a `[target] repository` names no repository or has no default branch, or `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>` already holds a repository the engine created, which has no origin to fetch | Check the name or URL against the repository; for a taken name, remove the directory or pick another `[target] name`, then rerun |
| `merge-unresolved` | A slice's merge into the integrated tree conflicted at paths the adapter's merge rules leave, and the model could not resolve them: one side removed a path or holds a link, a directory, or a file that is not text there, or the resolution was refused within the available rounds | Inspect the named paths in `.emery/vcs/worktrees/<slice>` and `.emery/vcs/integration`; rerun to build the slice again over the merged head, declare a merge rule in the adapter for a list file or a generated one, or re-run `/emery:specify` for a plan that keeps the two in one slice; the label stands at the last verified wave |
| `verify-failed` | The adapter's verify turn failed a check over the integrated tree after a wave merged | Inspect `.emery/vcs/integration`; rerun to rebuild the wave from the last verified head, or `git bisect` over `emery/<revision>` to attribute the failure to a slice |
| `secret-detected` | A path a slice's build or a wave's verification wrote carries a credential — a key file or `.npmrc` by name, a PEM block or a provider's token by content — so nothing of that change set was sealed | Inspect the named path in `.emery/vcs/worktrees/<slice>` or `.emery/vcs/integration`, remove the credential from where the build read it, then rerun; the label stands at the last verified wave |
| `refused` | The loader rejected the release (an invalid or pre-compiled artifact, a `digest` it does not resolve to, an export signature that does not match this binary's contract, or a namespace the binary routes nowhere) | Read the message; rebuild or update a stale copy in `~/.emery/adapters/`, or for an unrouted namespace `wkg get <reference> -o ~/.emery/adapters/` as the hint says |
| `unavailable` | The registry fetch for a release the store lacks failed (network, endpoint, or a missing exact version) | Check connectivity and that the exact version is published under its namespace, then rerun; a copy at `~/.emery/adapters/<namespace>_<name>@<version>.wasm` stands in |
| `spec-outdated` | The sealed revision or the staged candidate was written under an older grammar than this binary reads | Re-run `/emery:specify <adapter>` to regenerate |

### Reviewing the generated documents

- Read `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` as re-projected after the run, or run `emery show spec --staged` / `emery show design --staged` / `emery show plan --staged` for the candidate and `emery show spec` / `emery show design` / `emery show plan` for the sealed revision — the document renders to stdout with its revision id in the front matter
- `spec.md`, `design.md`, and `plan.md` are projections; none is edited by hand — change a source and re-run `/emery:specify`
- `spec/spec.json`, `spec/design.json`, and `spec/plan.json` in the `[target]` repository are the sealed revision, held to the id their label names; never edit them

